A||||8||ponya||Auto transmission fluid change||||5||
Z||000000||ponya||08-05-2000||07:27 PM||||Has anyone changed their auto trans fluid without dropping the pan. Years ago, we used to pump it out, or as much as you could get out. Anyone still do it that way? Just curious. I want to change mine, just don't want to drop the pan. Thanks, Bob||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||08-05-2000||07:40 PM||||You could do that, but part of the point of changing the fluid is to change the filter. You also want to clean the magnet in the pan which is likely covered to capacity in metal particles.||||reg||1
Z||000002||EdsB52||08-05-2000||07:47 PM||||I would, and have, dropped the pan, changed filter, drill a hole in the corner of the pan, have a castelated nut welded upside-down on the inside of pan, and reinstall with appropriate bolt and plastic washer. The obvious advantage is that you can drain the fluid in the future without having to drop the pan.<P>The inferior Japanese manufacturers have done this for years.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Terrybogin||08-05-2000||07:59 PM||||On my cars w/o a drainplug, I drill a small hole in the pan where the plug needs to be before dropping it to change fluid/filter. Then I let it drain for awhile and remove the empty pan, install the drainplug in my newly created hole and replace the filter. Makes cleanup a lot easier because you dont have to wrestle with trying to handle a full oil pan.||||reg||1
Z||000004||EdsB52||08-05-2000||09:19 PM||||Really, Terry Bogin. Then what do you call the mess created as the drill bit penetrates the trans pan and the fluid leaks all over your hand, the drill (hope it's not an electric drill), and the ground? Sounds like more work / risk than simply removing the pan. What do use as a drill stop, the transmission valve body?||||reg||1
Z||000005||olympic||08-05-2000||10:30 PM||||Heres one method.<P>1.  Remove the trasmission oil cooler line from the radiator which bring the oil from the tranny.  Attach to it a few feet of rubber hose putting the free end in a oil drain pan.<P>2.  Start the car and the tranny will pump out its oil into the drain pan.<P>3.  When the oils flow has slowed/stopped, shut the car off, remove the tranny pan(which should be almost empty) and change the filter, clean the magnet and replace the pan.<P>4.  pour 10 litres of ATF into the transmission.<P>5.  Raise the back of the car so the wheels can turn freely.  Start the car and put it in drive(dont rev the engine, let it idle).  Have a friend watch the oil that is being discharged and tell you when it is the new clean oil.  If the old oil in your trans is fairly clean, you may want to somehow measure how much oil you are pumping out to know when to stop.  You will want to pump out the capacity of your trans plus 1-2 litres.<P>6.  Shut off the car and re-attach the cooler line.  <P>7.  Restart engine, trans in neutral.  Check oil level and add/drain if necessary.<P>   If this is all too complicated/messy you can do what I do and pay the $80 every 100k miles to have your trans professionally flushed.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>"Those who can,  do.  Those who can't,  teach."||||reg||3
Z||000006||Terrybogin||08-05-2000||11:44 PM||||Whatever-<BR>If you are stupid enough to run the bit into the valve body then you get what you deserve. The hole is in the side of the pan, not the bottom. It works for me- if you want to handle 5 quarts of oil balanced in the pan, then go ahead. I put the drain pan under the spot where I drill, use an 1/4 bit with a travel stop, punch the hole quick and then go grab a sandwhich while it drains. Very simple and clean. Then I add a B/M bolt in drain plug kit so I dont have to try and weld the pan and cause it to warp and become a permanent source of oil in the floor. But I guess real mechanics just love to be covered in burnt tranny fluid and motor oil. It must drive the ladies wild Ed.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Terrybogin (edited 08-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||08-06-2000||12:37 AM||||All good ideas - no need to argue about which one is best - use whatever works for <B>you</B>.  <P>I wish I had read this thread a long time ago.  I always used to remove the auto transmission dipstick, picked the car up and inverted it so that the old transmission fluid poured out the dipstick tube like a pitcher, into an empty milk container.  Simple, huh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ?<P>..and then I woke up  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">!  ||||reg||12
Z||000008||theogre||08-06-2000||04:29 PM||||Actually I've done various pump down methods on auto trannys. I found with the TH125's that you can pump down nearly all of it. I got over 6.5 quarts into mine last time. If you pump down that far you don't need to flush.<P>The way I do it is to connect the drain hose to the tranny cooling outlet. (I always have to look up which line that is.)<P>start the car like he said above and let it pump down. Don't put the car in gear.<P>When flow stops shut off the car and let the it sit 10-15 minutes. then run it again.<P>That's all I do and the pan ends up close to dry. (I also blow out the cooler lines good while things are apart.)<P>I don't like flushing much unless it's really needed. It wastes an awfull lot of oil. If you do flush make sure you have someplace to take all that oil first. PLEASE don't dump it on the ground. ||||reg||1
